---
import DocsCodeBlock from "../../components/DocsCodeBlock.astro";
import DocsLayout from "../../components/DocsLayout.astro";
import DocsRelated from "../../components/DocsRelated.astro";

/* THE REPRODUCIBLE HALF OF A SANDBOX. This page owns the user-facing contract of sandbox.toml: what belongs,
 * what is intentionally absent, and why applying a file never imports somebody else's consent. The internal
 * portability classes and schema implementation belong in the repository design note, not in the operating
 * instructions a sandbox owner needs. */

const example = [
    "# Intentic sandbox definition: safe to review and publish.",
    "schemaVersion = 1",
    'name = "release-captain"',
    "",
    "# Names only. The target asks its owner for the values.",
    'secrets = ["SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN"]',
    "",
    "[environment]",
    'baseImage = "ghcr.io/intentic/sandbox:stable"',
    "dockerfile = '''",
    "RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends ffmpeg",
    "'''",
    "",
    "# Only choices that differ from the defaults.",
    "[settings]",
    "workspaceMap = true",
    "",
    "[[repositories]]",
    'id = "product"',
    'remote = "https://github.com/example/product.git"',
    'ref = "main"',
    "",
    "# The connection lands visibly unauthenticated.",
    "[[capabilities]]",
    'id = "linear"',
    'kind = "mcp"',
    'config = { url = "https://mcp.linear.app/sse" }',
].join("\n");

const contents = [
    { item: "Repositories", definition: "Workspace id, clone remote and optional branch.", arrival: "Cloned through the sandbox's ordinary repo path." },
    { item: "Connections", definition: "Capability id, kind and non-secret configuration.", arrival: "Listed as unauthenticated until this owner connects it." },
    { item: "Secrets", definition: "Names only.", arrival: "Shown as values this owner still needs to provide." },
    { item: "Environment", definition: "Base-image note and the approved custom Dockerfile source.", arrival: "Parked as a proposal at the target owner's approval gate." },
    { item: "Agent settings", definition: "Only choices that differ from schema defaults.", arrival: "Merged through the ordinary settings store." },
];
---

<DocsLayout
    pageId="sandbox-definitions"
    heading="Sandbox definitions"
    lead="A sandbox.toml is the reproducible shape of an environment: code by reference, connections without credentials, an owner-reviewed overlay recipe, and the agent settings that matter. It is designed to be committed, diffed and handed to somebody else."
>
    <h2>Definition, not snapshot</h2>
    <p>
        Some parts of a sandbox have a source that can be named again: a repo has a remote and branch, a connection has a kind and
        non-secret configuration, and an environment overlay is source code. Other parts exist only here: transcripts, unpublished
        branches, checkpoints, account sessions and ledgers. A definition carries the first set as references. A{" "}
        <strong>bundle</strong> carries the definition plus the irreplaceable state.
    </p>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Use</th><th>Sandbox definition</th><th>Sandbox bundle</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Purpose</strong></td>
                <td>Reproduce or review an environment.</td>
                <td>Move or restore this sandbox's state.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Format</strong></td>
                <td>Small deterministic TOML.</td>
                <td>Manifest plus an archive of carried files.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Safe to publish</strong></td>
                <td>Yes: no credential values or identity.</td>
                <td>No: treat it as a private backup.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><strong>Good for</strong></td>
                <td>Code review, drift checks, templates and stamping out a fleet.</td>
                <td>Continuity, migration and disaster recovery.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>What the TOML describes</h2>
    <DocsCodeBlock code={example} lang="toml" title="sandbox.toml" />
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Section</th><th>What travels</th><th>What happens on arrival</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {
                contents.map((entry) => (
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>{entry.item}</strong></td>
                        <td>{entry.definition}</td>
                        <td>{entry.arrival}</td>
                    </tr>
                ))
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
        The file does not include credentials, browser or provider sessions, owner identity, transcripts, automations, personas, locally
        authored skills, drafts, unpublished git data or checkpoints. An export also lists anything it could not express — for example a
        repository with no remote — instead of quietly leaving it out.
    </p>

    <h2>Why TOML</h2>
    <p>
        The artifact is a shallow manifest people edit and review. TOML gives the Dockerfile a readable multiline block without YAML's value
        coercion or JSON's escaped newline noise, and it does not turn the file into a program. Equal live state produces byte-identical
        output: section order and value spelling are deterministic, so a git diff is a real environment diff rather than formatter churn.
    </p>
    <p>
        The definition is <strong>derived, never kept as a second copy</strong>. Downloading one reads the live repo remotes, capability
        manifests, overlay and settings each time. There is no background job that can forget to update sandbox.toml while the real sandbox
        moves on.
    </p>

    <h2>Download, apply or compare</h2>
    <p>Open <strong>Sandbox → Environment → Sandbox definition</strong>. The three actions have deliberately different effects:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Download sandbox.toml</strong> derives the current file and names anything omitted from it.</li>
        <li>
            <strong>Apply a definition</strong> parses the file into a checklist. Applicable items start selected; existing repo paths and
            connection ids are marked “already here” and are never overwritten.
        </li>
        <li><strong>Compare against one</strong> writes nothing. It returns one line per difference, or says the sandbox is in agreement.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Apply is preview-first. The daemon holds the exact parsed file behind a short-lived plan token, then re-derives the selected items
        from those held bytes when you confirm. The browser cannot alter the reviewed plan on the way back.
    </p>

    <h2>Consent does not travel</h2>
    <p>
        A shared file may name an overlay that installs packages, but it does not carry the source owner's approval. Applying it writes an
        environment proposal; the target owner reviews and rebuilds it through the same gate as an agent-proposed change. Connections arrive
        without authentication and secret names arrive without values. The apply report repeats every manual step that remains.
    </p>
    <p>
        Existing things are protected by the same rule: a definition lands <strong>beside, never over</strong>. It is an additive arrival
        mechanism, not a force-convergence command. Use Compare to measure drift and make an explicit local change when the target already has
        a conflicting repo or connection.
    </p>

    <h2>One definition, many sandboxes</h2>
    <p>
        The sandbox run contract can seed an empty workspace from the TOML on first boot. Repositories are cloned, connection cards are
        created unauthenticated, settings are applied, and the overlay waits for its new owner. Replaying the same container environment on a
        later rebuild is inert because the workspace is no longer empty. That makes one reviewed definition a fleet recipe without turning
        boot into an unreviewed configuration executor.
    </p>
    <p>
        <a href="/docs/remote-runners/">Runners</a> reuse the same definition contract more narrowly. They receive the parent's approved image
        overlay and a settings-only definition; repos still travel through the conversation's git branches, and capabilities and secrets do
        not travel at all.
    </p>

    <DocsRelated
        links={[
            { href: "/docs/docker/", title: "Docker setup", why: "the replaceable image and durable volumes a definition describes." },
            { href: "/docs/remote-runners/", title: "Remote runners", why: "how the definition keeps execution environments in step." },
            { href: "/docs/architecture/", title: "Architecture", why: "why reproducible shape and irreplaceable state are separate." },
        ]}
    />
</DocsLayout>
